## More ways to make your phone harder to steal

- **Remove airplane mode from Quick Settings**, so a thief can't cut the connection with one swipe. See [how to stop thieves from turning off your phone](/blog/block-power-menu).
- **Lock your SIM card with a PIN** (`Settings > Security & privacy > More security settings > SIM lock`), so a thief can't use your number to receive your codes.
- **Hide notification content on the lock screen** (`Settings > Security & privacy > More security settings > Notifications on lock screen`), so security codes don't show while the phone is locked.
- **Keep your phone updated**, since new theft protection features arrive with Android updates.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### Q: What is the most important anti-theft setting on Android?
A: A strong screen lock. A 6-digit PIN or a password protects everything else; if a thief can guess your lock, they can turn off the rest.

### Q: Does Android have built-in theft protection?
A: Yes. Theft protection includes Theft Detection Lock, Offline Device Lock and Failed Authentication Lock, and you can add Remote Lock and Identity Check. Find them under Settings > Google > All services > Theft protection.

### Q: Do these settings work on Samsung phones?
A: Google's Theft protection works on most Android phones, including Samsung. Samsung Galaxy phones also have their own Theft protection menu under Settings > Security and privacy > Lost device protection.

### Q: Can a thief still turn off my phone?
A: Sometimes, from the lock screen. Some phones, including many Samsung models, can require your PIN to turn off. Read [how to stop thieves from turning off your phone](/blog/block-power-menu).

### Q: Do I need an anti-theft app if I use these settings?
A: The settings protect your data and help you locate the phone. They can't show you who tried to unlock it. An app like CrookCatcher adds a photo, the location and an email alert at the moment someone tries.

